<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-uniform-limits-of-uniformly-continuous-maps" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Uniform limits of uniformly continuous maps
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> be a sequence of uniformly continuous maps <m> f_{ n } \colon X \to Y </m> from a metric space <m> \of{ X , \rho_{ X } } </m> to a metric space <m> \of{ Y , \rho_{ Y } } </m>. Prove that if <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly, the uniform limit is also uniformly continuous.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <!-- <solution>
    <p>
      Let <m> f = \uniflim_{ n \to \infty }{ f_{ n } } </m> be the uniform limit of the sequence <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m>, and consider a positive real number <m> \epsilon \gt 0 </m>. Since <m> \of{ f_{ n } }_{ n = 0 }^{ \infty } </m> converges uniformly to <m> f </m>, there is a natural number <m> n_{ \epsilon } \in \N </m> so that
      
      <md>
        \rho_{ Y } \of{ f_{ n } \of{ x } , f \of{ x } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
      </md>
      
      for all indices <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m> and points <m> x \in X </m>. Fix one such index <m> n \geq n_{ \epsilon } </m>; for example, we may choose <m> n = n_{ \epsilon } </m>. Since <m> f_{ n } </m> is uniformly continuous, there is a positive real number <m> \delta \gt 0 </m> so that
      
      <md>
        \rho_{ Y } \of{ f_{ n } \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f_{ n } \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 }
      </md>
      
      for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m> so that <m> \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta </m>. In particular, we observe that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } } \amp \leq \rho_{ Y } \of{ f \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f_{ n } \of{ x_{ 1 } } } + \rho_{ Y } \of{ f_{ n } \of{ x_{ 1 } } , f_{ n } \of{ x_{ 2 } } } + \rho_{ Y } \of{ f_{ n } \of{ x_{ 2 } } , f \of{ x_{ 2 } } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp \lt \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } + \frac{ \epsilon }{ 3 } = \epsilon
        </mrow>
      </md>
      
      for all points <m> x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } \in X </m> so that <m> \rho_{ X } \of{ x_{ 1 } , x_{ 2 } } \lt \delta </m>. Thus <m> f </m> is uniformly continuous.
    </p>
  </solution> -->
</exercise>
